    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d301">

                <pb facs="0467" n="449" sameAs="frus:frus1946v05/pg449"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.00/5–846</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State, at
                        Paris</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-08">May 8, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Various Frenchmen have remarked that your firm stand during the Conference<anchor
                        n="62" xml:id="d301-f1"/> in the face of Soviet pretensions was a
                    contributing factor in persuading many voters to go to the polls last Sunday.
                    Those voters were of the category who had about come to the conclusion <pb
                        facs="0468" n="450" xml:id="pg450"/> that no one was going to stand up to
                    Molotov and that consequently the Cossacks would soon be arriving on the Place
                    de la Concorde. Your firm stand convinced them that perhaps the Cossacks won’t
                    get here after all.</p>
                <p>Respectfully</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jefferson Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
